#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056014 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the equation of the **horizontal line** that passes through the point $(0,3)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/37/379af2ba930fa537f1fbdda5b4b5cc91e355a46e90918cdc56bbce66f3502d7b.png)\n\nNotice that for *any* $x$-value on the line, the $y$-value is always $\\color{red}3.$ \n\n![](images/ff/ff5ec246a558fc981ba5f0b0085ce3663ac6ab837121f31a5d44de6adb91cb90.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=3.$\n\n**Note**: When writing the equation of a horizontal line, we always put the variable $y$ on the left side and the constant on the right side." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d036bf9-1cb2-59e4-a418-b586ec35d56e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8070 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to find the equation of the **vertical line** that passes through the point $(-4,0)$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/06/065dfc149c1624bbe2c6fd51085c48b5e60c1d37d248d073dd1374d08e65b5af.png)\n\nNotice that for *any* $y$-value on the line, the $x$-value is always $\\color{red}-4.$ \n\n![](images/01/01d24274cc3d2b5a7899cc1aa8cee4aef4fb02ddb45acd27629a57d6af41a398.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of this vertical line is $x=-4.$\n\n**Note**: When writing the equation of a vertical line, we always put the variable $x$ on the left side and the constant on the right side." :tutorial/id #uuid "fcdbdf94-22ce-5781-9f56-42dc93763172" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8071 :tutorial/title "Vertical Lines"}]}
